{#
  THE OUTCOME OF A STAND APPLICATION — offered, not selected, waiting list, or expired.

  ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ────────────────────────────────────────────

  It did not, and the consequence was the most serious defect in the vendor-stands
  subsystem. A decision was recorded in `gates_stand_applications` and NOBODY WAS TOLD.
  An offer carries a {{ '' }}72-hour acceptance clock (StandApplication::OFFER_HOURS) that started
  running the moment an organiser pressed Record; the vendor could only discover it by
  logging in unprompted, and `Maintenance::expireStandOffers()` then released the pitch to
  the waiting list — silently, again.

  Meanwhile the public application page promises, in its own words: "You hear either way,
  with a reason." So the platform was making a specific promise and keeping none of it,
  and the people it broke were small businesses who had gathered certificates for it.

  ── ONE TEMPLATE, FOUR OUTCOMES ────────────────────────────────────────────

  Not four files. The four messages differ in one headline, one paragraph, and whether
  there is a button — everything else is identical, and four copies of an inbox-compatible
  skeleton is four places for a rendering fix to be applied three times.

  The `kind` variable drives it. `offered` is the only one with a deadline block and a
  button, because it is the only one where the reader has something to do and a clock.

  ── AND WHY THE SKELETON IS COPIED RATHER THAN INVENTED ────────────────────

  Every <head> and wrapper decision here was paid for once in emails/questionnaire.twig
  and is not worth re-deriving: fluid-hybrid width with an MSO conditional for Outlook
  (which ignores max-width), a hosted PNG mark rather than a data: URI (Gmail, Outlook and
  Yahoo all refuse data: in an img src), `mso-line-height-rule: exactly` so Word does not
  collapse long paragraphs, and a styled preheader.

  ── THE REASON IS QUOTED, NOT PARAPHRASED ──────────────────────────────────

  A rejection is refused server-side without one, and what the organiser typed is what the
  vendor reads. Summarising it here would put words in the panel's mouth about a decision
  somebody will ask about.
#}
{#
  The questionnaire invitation.

  ── WHY IT IS NOT THE PLAIN-TEXT NOTE IT USED TO BE ────────────────────────

  This went out through `OtpService::sendCustom`, which is `isHTML(false)`. So the one
  message on the platform that asks a nominee to do real work — write about themselves,
  gather documents, upload them — arrived looking like a system notice, next to a
  branded voting broadcast that only asked them to share a link. The ask that needs the
  most trust had the least of it.

  ── AND WHY IT REUSES final-hours.twig's SKELETON ──────────────────────────

  Every inbox-compatibility decision in the <head> and wrapper of that file was paid for
  once and is not worth re-deriving: fluid-hybrid width with an MSO conditional for
  Outlook (which ignores max-width), a hosted PNG mark rather than a data: URI (Gmail,
  Outlook and Yahoo all refuse data: in an img src), an explicit dark palette rather than
  a defensive one, `mso-line-height-rule: exactly` so Word does not collapse long
  paragraphs, and a styled preheader. Diverging from it would mean discovering the same
  things again in somebody's Outlook.

  ── WHAT IS DIFFERENT, DELIBERATELY ────────────────────────────────────────

  · No countdown GIF. A ticking clock is right for "voting closes in six hours" and
    wrong for a task that takes an evening — it says hurry to somebody who needs to go
    and find a letter from 2019. The deadline is a line of text.
  · The deadline block renders ONLY when there is one, and never claims the form locks.
    It does not: the questionnaire stays open afterwards. Saying otherwise to hurry
    somebody along would be a lie the platform then contradicts.
  · The two fraud/honesty lines from the old plain-text body are kept verbatim in
    substance. They are the most important sentences in the message — "nothing here
    costs money" is what protects a nominee from somebody impersonating us — so they are
    set as real content, not footer small print.
#}
